it's been nearly two months since five  people were shot and killed in this  residential area of plymouth  now the police watchdog says it's  investigating an officer and staff  member at devon and cornwell police over  their handling of jake davison's shotgun  license and previous assaults  in a statement the independent office  for police conduct said after reviewing  information obtained so far we have now  issued a gross misconduct notice to a  member of police staff and a misconduct  notice to a police officer  we are investigating the police staff  members handling of mr davison's  original application for a shotgun  certificate in july 2017  as well as their decision to return mr  davidson's shotgun and shotgun  certificate in july this year  separately a police officer is facing a  misconduct notice over how they handled  assaults on two young people by davidson  last september which led to the 22 year  old's weapon being seized we know that  it wasn't the police that pulled the  trigger and committed those murders but  we also know that if there were failings  then people will need to be held  accountable for that but in my mind we  need to look bigger than just whether  devon and corn will follow the right  procedures  this is a situation that now needs top  to bottom review of gun licensing we  need to have a better understanding of  social media better understanding of  mental health as part of giving out  certificates devon and cornwall police  have acknowledged the watchdogs interim  findings and say the staff member and  officer involved have not been suspended  and that the issuing of such misconduct  notices does not necessarily mean that  disciplinary action will be taken but  the issuing of a shotgun to davidson in  2017 and the weapon's subsequent return  just weeks before the attack are clearly  areas of serious concern  davidson had made online posts  discussing firearms law and this ongoing  report raises the question over whether  checks are strong enough  anything involving an arrest or  an allegation  of a violent or aggressive nature  will normally be a trigger to the police  refusing the person's application or  refusing to renew their certificate  where  an individual has their certificate  seized and their suitability is being  reviewed  the kind of steps the police will take  may well include  looking at the individual social media  output the iopc says its report into  devon and cornwall police will be  completed by the end of this year  dan white said sky news in plymouth
 

